  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/l90a-panaque-bathyphilus-2/",
      "html": "<p>The large-growing, magnificent Panaque bathyphilus is very variable in coloration. Specimens 16-20 cm long are about half grown, as the maximum length given for P. bathyphilus is about 60 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/l90a-panaque-bathyphilus-2/",
      "html": "<p>P. bathyphilus occurs in Peru (surroundings of Tingo Maria).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/l90a-panaque-bathyphilus-2/",
      "html": "<p>These large fish should only be kept in large aquariums with strong filtration.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/l90a-panaque-bathyphilus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Like all Panaque, P. bathyphilus is predominantly a wood eater. The amount of feces produced by this nutrient-poor diet is enormous, which is another reason why large tanks with strong filtration are necessary.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/l90a-panaque-bathyphilus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Among themselves Panaque are not quite without aggression and can become quite rough. However, L90 is considered one of the more tolerant species in this regard.</p>"
    }
  ],
